"[1610.04769] Optimal sampling rates for approximating analytic functions from pointwise samples (https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.04769)\nciteturn0view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.04769\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 164\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Mathematics > Numerical Analysis\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 15 Oct 2016 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 5 Apr 2018 (this version, v3)]\nL9: # Title:Optimal sampling rates for approximating analytic functions from pointwise samples\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Ben Adcock , cite7†Rodrigo Platte , cite8†Alexei Shadrin L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Optimal sampling rates for approximating analytic functions from pointwise samples, by Ben Adcock and 1 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite9†View PDF cite10†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:We consider the problem of approximating an analytic function on a compact interval from its values at $M+1$ distinct points. When the points are equispaced, a recent result (the so-called impossibility theorem) has shown that the best possible convergence rate of a stable method is root-exponential in $M$, and that any method with faster exponential convergence must also be exponentially ill-conditioned at a certain rate.\nL17: This result hinges on a classical theorem of Coppersmith & Rivlin concerning the maximal behaviour of polynomials bounded on an equispaced grid. In this paper, we first generalize this theorem to arbitrary point distributions. We then present an extension of the impossibility theorem valid for general nonequispaced points, and apply it to the case of points that are equidistributed with respect to (modified) Jacobi weight functions.\nL18: This leads to a necessary sampling rate for stable approximation from such points. We prove that this rate is also sufficient, and therefore exactly quantify (up to constants) the precise sampling rate for approximating analytic functions from such node distributions with stable methods. Numerical results -- based on computing the maximal polynomial via a variant of the classical Remez algorithm -- confirm our main theorems.\nL19: Finally, we discuss the implications of our results for polynomial least-squares approximations. In particular, we theoretically confirm the well-known heuristic that stable least-squares approximation using polynomials of degree $N < M$ is possible only once $M$ is sufficiently large for there to be a subset of $N$ of the nodes that mimic the behaviour of the $N$th set of Chebyshev nodes.\nL20: Subjects:  | Numerical Analysis (math.NA)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite11†arXiv:1610.04769 [math.NA]\nL22:    | (or cite12†arXiv:1610.04769v3 [math.NA] for this version)\nL23:    | cite13†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1610.04769†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Ben Adcock [cite14†view email ]\nL27: cite5†[v1] Sat, 15 Oct 2016 18:07:53 UTC (78 KB)\nL28: cite15†[v2] Mon, 30 Jan 2017 20:11:47 UTC (503 KB)\nL29: [v3] Thu, 5 Apr 2018 23:52:46 UTC (551 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled Optimal sampling rates for approximating analytic functions from pointwise samples, by Ben Adcock and 1 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite9†View PDF L38:   * cite10†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite16†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite17†view license L42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: math.NA\nL45: \nL46: cite18†< prev |   cite19†next > L47: \nL48: cite20†new | cite21†recent | cite22†2016-10 L49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: cite23†cs L53: cite24†cs.NA L54: cite25†math L55: \nL56: ### Referen